post_install() {
  cat <<'MSG'
==> lagd needs two things before it will do anything:

    1. Join the `input` group, then log out and back in:

         sudo usermod -aG input "$USER"

       This grants read access to every keyboard and mouse on the machine, and
       write access to /dev/uinput. That is what the input stage is, not an
       incidental requirement.

    2. Enable the stages you want, as *user* units — not system ones:

         systemctl --user enable --now lagd-input
         systemctl --user enable --now lagd-audio          # needs a --target, see below
         systemctl --user enable --now lagd-present-seed

==> Then dial them, independently and while they run:

      lagd-ctl status
      lagd-ctl set input 50
      lagd-ctl drop audio present     # out of the path entirely, not merely zero
      lagd-ctl toggle input
      lagd-ctl panic                  # everything off, if something feels wrong

==> Configuration lives in /etc/lagd/*.conf, read by the units as arguments.
    Set audio.conf's --target to your real sink (`wpctl status`) before enabling
    lagd-audio: without it the delayed output autoconnects to the default sink,
    which is a feedback loop if lagd is that default.

==> The present stage is a Vulkan layer, gated so it is not loaded into every
    process. Enable it per-program:

      LAGD_PRESENT=1 some-game

    Note that a present delay costs framerate as well as latency — see
    /usr/share/doc/lagd-git/README.md.
MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}

pre_remove() {
  # The input stage holds exclusive grabs on real devices. The kernel releases
  # them when the process dies either way, but stopping it first means the
  # devices come back before the binary goes away.
  if command -v loginctl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo '==> Stop the user units first if they are running:'
    echo '      systemctl --user disable --now lagd-input lagd-audio lagd-present-seed'
  fi
}
